      "evidence": "In find_user, the username value is read directly from the user-controlled HTTP query parameter via request.args.get('username', '') and concatenated into the SQL string with \"SELECT id, email FROM users WHERE name = '\" + username + \"'\". The resulting string is passed to db.execute(query) with no parameterization, sanitization, or escaping. Because the attacker controls the username string and it is placed inside single quotes that they can close, they can break out of the literal and inject arbitrary SQL. For example, supplying username=\"x' OR '1'='1' --\" yields SELECT id, email FROM users WHERE name = 'x' OR '1'='1' --' which returns every user row, and UNION-based payloads (e.g. \"' UNION SELECT password_hash, id FROM credentials --\") enable arbitrary data exfiltration. This is distinct from the query in app/reports.py:recent_report, which uses the parameterized form db.execute('SELECT * FROM reports WHERE owner = ?', (owner,)) where the driver binds owner as data via a ? placeholder, so it is not exploitable and is correctly omitted. Only the find_user path is exploitable.",
      "remediation": "Replace string concatenation with a parameterized query so user input is bound as data, not SQL. Change the function to: def find_user(request, db): username = request.args.get('username', ''); return db.execute('SELECT id, email FROM users WHERE name = ?', (username,)).fetchone(). Never interpolate user-controlled values into SQL text; always use placeholder binding for every parameterized column value."
    }
  ],
  "scanned_files": ["app/users.py", "app/reports.py"],
  "source_modified": false
}
"}

